  "people": {
    "heading": "Kings, teachers and poets",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "dharmapala",
        "name": "Dharmapala",
        "native": "ধর্মপাল",
        "life": "r. c. 770 to 810",
        "role": "Second Pala king, who made the dynasty a great power.",
        "steps": [
          1,
          2,
          3
        ],
        "text": "Gopala’s son fought the Pratiharas and Rashtrakutas for Kannauj, lost to both, yet kept an empire across Bengal and Bihar. His copper plates call him supreme lord. A devoted Buddhist, he founded Vikramashila and Somapura, two of the greatest monasteries of his age, and Tibetan histories remember him as a patron of learning."
      },
      {
        "id": "devapala",
        "name": "Devapala",
        "native": "দেবপাল",
        "life": "r. c. 810 to 850",
        "role": "Pala king under whom the empire was at its height.",
        "steps": [
          4
        ],
        "text": "Dharmapala’s son ruled for about forty years. His inscriptions claim conquests from the Himalayas to the Vindhyas; how much is true is disputed. His Nalanda copper plate records his grant to a monastery built by a Sailendra king of Sumatra and Java, a sign of the links between Pala India and Buddhist Southeast Asia."
      },
      {
        "id": "atisha",
        "name": "Atisha Dipankara Shrijnana",
        "native": "অতীশ দীপঙ্কর",
        "life": "982 to 1054",
        "role": "Teacher at Vikramashila who went to Tibet.",
        "steps": [
          6
        ],
        "text": "Born in Bengal, by tradition at Vikrampur near Dhaka, he studied in India and, according to his biographies, in Sumatra. He became a leading teacher at Vikramashila before going to Tibet, where he wrote the Lamp for the Path to Enlightenment. He died near Lhasa in 1054 and is honoured in Tibet and Bangladesh."
      },
      {
        "id": "sandhyakara-nandi",
        "name": "Sandhyakara Nandi",
        "native": "সন্ধ্যাকর নন্দী",
        "life": "11th to 12th century",
        "role": "Poet of the Ramacharita.",
        "steps": [
          7
        ],
        "text": "A court poet whose father served Ramapala, he wrote the Ramacharita, a Sanskrit poem in which every verse carries two meanings, one telling the Ramayana and one telling Ramapala’s recovery of Varendra. It is the main source for the Kaivarta revolt. Only one manuscript survives, found in Nepal in the 19th century."
      }
    ]
  }
